  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Primary path: upload to reviewed result",
      "items": [
        "1. Browser uploads text or a file through nginx; API validates identity, input, workspace ownership and credits.",
        "2. API commits the document, pending conversion and durable job together; returns an accepted response.",
        "3. Worker polls PostgreSQL, acquires a lease, decrypts the source and calls the conversion engine.",
        "4. Engine calls the configured LLM; worker commits the encrypted result and usage under the held lease.",
        "5. Browser polls the API every 2 seconds until done or failed, then supports human review and export."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "Trust and document handling",
      "items": [
        "Detected personal data requires explicit acknowledgment at intake; the runtime prompt receives document text. Do not infer automatic redaction from the Compose masking comment.",
        "Document source, original file bytes and converted text are encrypted at application storage boundaries.",
        "Bearer authentication and ownership checks protect document access. nginx enforces a 10 MB upload limit.",
        "External LLMs process document content; OAuth and payment providers serve separate identity and billing paths."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Execution and persistence",
      "items": [
        "PostgreSQL holds durable conversion jobs and results; a separate broker is not required by this path.",
        "API and worker are separate processes. The conversion engine is a logical role inside the worker, not another service.",
        "The engine adds dictionary context, checks style and fact preservation, and may run one repair pass.",
        "Result, usage ledger and credit settlement commit together only while the worker still holds its lease."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Supporting integrations and scope",
      "items": [
        "Configured OAuth providers: Google, Kakao and Naver. Toss handles billing-key issuance, charges and refunds.",
        "NAVER Cloud SENS sends phone OTPs; SMTP can deliver verification and completion mail. These supporting paths remain in cards.",
        "Supporting adapters: SensSmsSender.send (lines 54–83) and SmtpMailSender.send (31–40) in backend-kotlin/infrastructure.",
        "Scope: committed source at 34622c1 plus compose.yml. Local ports bind to loopback; production ingress and active vendor settings are not established here."
      ]
    }
  ]
